      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "63f0a3c8-0658-5188-afdc-608bae46fcac",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Bogribari, Dhubri, Bilasipara, Guwahati, Nilachal Hill, Lower Assam, Assam, India\n- Organizations: Mahamaya Dham, Kamakhya Temple\n- Concepts: Shakti Peethas, Shakti Pithas, Mahavidyas, Hindu pilgrimage, tourism\n\nFacts:\n- Mahamaya Dham, also called the Mahamaya Temple, is located in Bogribari about 30 km east of Dhubri town and 10 km west of Bilasipara town in Lower Assam, India.\n- Mahamaya Dham is regarded as one of the greatest Shakti Peethas for Hindu pilgrims in Lower Assam, India.\n- Mahamaya Dham is considered second only to Kamakhya Temple of Guwahati as an attraction for pilgrims and tourists.\n- Kamakhya Temple is one of the oldest of the 51 Shakti Pithas.\n- Kamakhya Temple is situated on Nilachal Hill in the western part of Guwahati, Assam, India.\n- Kamakhya Temple is the main temple in a complex of individual temples dedicated to the ten Mahavidyas: Kali, Tara, Sodashi, Bhuvaneshwari, Bhairavi, Chhinnamasta, Dhumavati, Bagalamukhi, Matangi, and Kamala.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "396a80f3-38bf-59b7-9983-7c1b5e351c89",
        "text": "Page: Mahamaya Dham. Sentence: Mahamaya Dham or Mahamaya (supreme illusion) Temple of Bogribari, about 30 km east from Dhubri town and 10 km west from Bilasipara town, is regarded as a greatest Shakti Peethas for Hindu pilgrims in Lower Assam, India.\nPage: Mahamaya Dham. Sentence: In terms of attractions, this temple is the second to Kamakhya Temple of Guwahati to the pilgrims and tourists.\nPage: Kamakhya Temple. Sentence: It is one of the oldest of the 51 Shakti Pithas. Situated on the Nilachal Hill in western part of Guwahati city in Assam, India, it is the main temple in a complex of individual temples dedicated to the ten Mahavidyas: Kali, Tara, Sodashi, Bhuvaneshwari, Bhairavi, Chhinnamasta, Dhumavati, Bagalamukhi, Matangi and Kamala.",
